.TH CYPACHE 8 2026-10-30 Cypache "Cypache Manual"
.ds AD l
.nh
.SH NAME
cypache \- secure, fast HTTP server for Python
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B systemctl
.RB { start | stop | restart | reload | status }
.B cypache
.PP
.B /opt/cypache/venv/bin/python \-I
.I config.py
.SH DESCRIPTION
Cypache serves static files and Python applications over HTTP/1.1 and, with TLS, HTTP/2.
It is written in Cython on top of fastepoll, a multi\-process epoll server core:
each worker process runs an event loop and the sites' Python handlers, natively, with no CGI or FastCGI hop.
.PP
Its configuration is a Python script that builds the sites and calls
.BR serve() ,
described in
.BR cypache\-config (5).
The Python side of a site, its handlers, apps and views, is in
.BR cypache\-apps (7).
Installed as a service, it runs
.I /etc/cypache/config.py
as the
.B cypache
user, with the hardened systemd unit
.BR cypache.service ,
on ports 80 and 443.
.PP
Cypache is strict by design.
A request that a proxy or firewall in front could read differently is refused, or redirected to its one clean form;
static files are confined to their root by the kernel;
and every limit and timeout has a safe default and can't be turned off.
.SH SITES
The default configuration serves each folder in
.I /var/www/sites
as a site named after its host:
.PP
.EX
/var/www/sites/example.com/public/        static files
/var/www/sites/example.com/controllers/   Python handlers, routed by path
/var/www/sites/example.com/views/         their views
/var/www/sites/example.com/example/       the site\(aqs own package
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
A folder answers for its name and for
.B www.\&
its name; one whose name starts with
.B www.\&
or is an address in brackets answers for that name alone.
.IP \(bu 2
A folder named
.BR default ,
or a symlink to a site's folder, answers for every other name, also for domains others point at the server.
Without one, a request for an unknown name gets
.BR "421 Misdirected Request" .
Until there's a site at all, a page says Cypache works.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.I lost+found
folder is no site.
Any other folder whose name isn't a host name stops the configuration with an error.
.IP \(bu 2
The sites' folders are on one Python import path, so a site's package (or module) needs a name of its own.
One that another site's package, or one of Python's or the installed ones, already has stops the configuration with an error,
rather than let one site's
.B import
load another's code.
.IP \(bu 2
A site is served over HTTPS as soon as its certificate is in
.IR /etc/cypache/tls/ name
(see HTTPS); plain HTTP then redirects there.
.PP
A first site, with a page and a handler for
.IR /news :
.PP
.EX
mkdir \-p /var/www/sites/example.com/public /var/www/sites/example.com/controllers
echo "<h1>Hello</h1>" > /var/www/sites/example.com/public/index.html
printf \(aqdef get(request):\en  return "<h1>News</h1>"\en\(aq > /var/www/sites/example.com/controllers/news.py
.EE
.SH CHANGES
A changed or new
.BR .py ,
the configuration's included, is served a second or so after it's saved.
The new code loads in a new generation of the server while the previous one keeps serving;
code that doesn't load is reported in the journal, and the previous code goes on serving.
Static files are served as they are at once, and a new site's folder, or a site's new
.IR public/ ,
.I controllers/
or
.IR views/ ,
is picked up without a restart too.
.PP
Other changes take a command:
.TP
.B systemctl restart cypache
after changing the ports or addresses the configuration listens on.
.TP
.B systemctl reload cypache
loads new certificates (SIGHUP) for new connections; open ones keep theirs.
The certbot deploy hook does this by itself.
.TP
.B systemctl status cypache
shows whether it runs.
A configuration that fails at a start is restarted again and again, ever more slowly, and its errors are in the journal:
.BR "journalctl \-u cypache" .
.SH HTTPS
Get a certificate once a site answers over plain HTTP:
.PP
.EX
certbot certonly \-\-webroot \-w /var/www/acme \-d example.com \-d www.example.com \e
  \-\-deploy\-hook /opt/cypache/tools/certbot\-deploy\-hook
.EE
.PP
Certbot's keys are readable by root only.
The deploy hook checks that certificate and key match, copies them for the
.B cypache
group into a new directory, and switches the symlink
.IR /etc/cypache/tls/ name
to it in one rename, so the server never reads a certificate and key from different renewals.
Then it has Cypache reload its certificates, or restart for a name's first certificate.
Pass it per certificate as above: in certbot's
.I renewal\-hooks/deploy
it would copy every certificate's key for the
.B cypache
group, also those of other services.
.PP
Over HTTPS, every response carries
.B Strict\-Transport\-Security
for a year, and plain HTTP gets
.B 308
to the same URL over HTTPS, except certbot's challenges in
.IR /var/www/acme .
TLS is 1.2 and 1.3, with forward\-secret AEAD ciphers only, and post\-quantum hybrid key exchange where OpenSSL has it.
On Linux 6.14 and later, with the kernel's
.B tls
module (the package loads it at boot), files go out with zero\-copy
.BR sendfile ()
through kernel TLS.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGTERM\fR, \fPSIGINT
Stop: open requests get
.B shutdownTimeout
(10 seconds) to finish, idle connections close at once.
systemd kills what's left after 20 seconds.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Load the certificates anew.
.TP
.B SIGQUIT
Stop at once, without waiting for requests.
.PP
The signals go to the main process, which passes them on to the server.
.SH SECURITY
.IP \(bu 2
The unit runs the server as
.BR cypache ,
with no privilege but binding ports 80 and 443, and no other ports
.RB ( SocketBindAllow= ).
It sees the system read\-only and no home directories, except
.I /var/log/cypache
and its own private
.IR /tmp ,
.I /var/tmp
and
.IR /dev/shm .
It can't see other processes or devices, and has the system calls of
.B @system\-service
without privileged ones.
A config listening on other ports changes
.BR SocketBindAllow= ,
with
.BR "systemctl edit cypache" .
.IP \(bu 2
Code and static files belong to root and are writable by no one else, nor are the directories above them:
a
.B .py
the server's user could plant would run within a second.
The package refuses to install where
.IR /opt ,
.IR /etc/cypache ,
.I /var/www/acme
or
.I /var/www/sites
isn't root's or is writable by others.
.IP \(bu 2
A directory an application writes to, such as one for uploads, belongs to
.B cypache
with mode 750, outside the static root and the code, and is listed in
.B ReadWritePaths=
.RB ( "systemctl edit cypache" ).
Everywhere else, writes fail with
.BR EROFS .
.IP \(bu 2
The server's processes aren't dumpable: other processes of its user can't attach to them or read their memory or open files.
Debug or profile them as root.
.IP \(bu 2
Every site's code runs in the same worker processes, and those hold every site's TLS private key.
Code one site runs can read the other sites' data and keys:
sites that don't trust each other belong on separate servers, each with a Cypache of its own.
.IP \(bu 2
Slow and idle clients are cut off by timeouts and minimum rates, in both directions; see
.BR cypache\-config (5).
.SH LOGS
Each response gets a line in
.IR /var/log/cypache/access.log ,
in the combined format followed by the Host and the seconds the response took;
.BR cypache\-config (5)
describes it.
It's rotated daily and kept for 14 days.
Errors, handler tracebacks and stuck handlers go to the journal:
.BR "journalctl \-u cypache" .
.SH PYTHON LIBRARIES
The server runs the system's Python, and its venv sees the system's packages, so a site's libraries come from apt with the system's security updates:
.PP
.EX
apt install python3\-pymysql python3\-pil python3\-bcrypt
.EE
.PP
A library the distribution doesn't package goes into the venv, and is then updated by hand:
.PP
.EX
/opt/cypache/venv/bin/python \-I \-m pip install \fIname\fP
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/cypache/config.py
The configuration, root's (mode 644, or 640 with group
.B cypache
if it holds secrets).
Upgrades keep changes to it.
.TP
.IR /etc/cypache/tls/ name /
The certificates the deploy hook copies, as
.I fullchain.pem
and
.IR privkey.pem .
.TP
.I /var/www/sites/
The default configuration's sites.
.TP
.I /var/www/acme/
Certbot's webroot, served for
.I /.well\-known/acme\-challenge/
over plain HTTP.
.TP
.I /var/log/cypache/access.log
The access log.
.TP
.I /opt/cypache/venv/
The server, fastepoll and their Python environment.
.TP
.I /opt/cypache/tools/certbot\-deploy\-hook
The certbot deploy hook.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/cypache.service
The unit; change it with
.BR "systemctl edit cypache" .
.TP
.I /etc/logrotate.d/cypache
The access log's rotation.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/cypache/README.md
The complete documentation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cypache\-config (5),
.BR cypache\-apps (7),
.BR certbot (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR systemd.exec (5)
